# ## 10. Final Business Insights and Conclusions
# 
# After doing this full analysis, here are the main things we found:
# 
# ---
# 
# **1. Free games attract more players**  
# Free-to-play games have significantly higher ownership numbers. The zero-cost barrier makes it easy for players to try the game. This was confirmed by our T-test (p < 0.05).
# 
# **2. Multiplayer increases engagement**  
# Games with multiplayer support have higher average playtime. This makes sense because multiplayer games give players a reason to keep coming back.
# 
# **3. Indie and Action are the most common genres**  
# But being common doesn't mean being successful. High competition in these genres makes discoverability harder.
# 
# **4. Steam grew rapidly after 2012**  
# The introduction of Steam Greenlight and later Steam Direct made it much easier to publish games. This led to a massive increase in game releases each year.
# 
# **5. Most games are priced under $20**  
# The median game price is around $5–$10. Very expensive games are rare and usually from big publishers.
# 
# **6. Review sentiment is generally positive**  
# Most games have a positive review ratio above 0.7. Bad games tend to get abandoned and don't accumulate many reviews.
# 
# **7. A few developers dominate the platform**  
# Steam follows a power law — a small number of developers have a huge share of total ownership.
# 
# ---
# 
# ## 11. Limitations
# 
# 1. Ownership numbers are estimated ranges, not exact values
# 2. We used midpoint of ownership ranges which introduces some error
# 3. Revenue data is not available — we can only estimate
# 4. The dataset is a snapshot and doesn't show how metrics change over time
# 5. Some games might have fake or manipulated reviews
# 
# ---
# 
